24
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:22,260
Before we dive into the
molecular physiology of

25
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:25,620
endothelial cells, let's
first ground ourselves in

26
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:28,280
the structure of the
vascular system and how blood

27
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:32,360
flows through it. The
circulatory system is composed

28
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:35,680
of three main types of
blood vessels, arteries,

29
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:38,020
veins, and capillaries.

30
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:41,940
Arteries carry oxygenated
blood away from the

31
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:44,660
heart under high pressure,
and they have thicker

32
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:47,740
walls with more smooth
muscle and elastic

33
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:51,480
tissue to withstand and
regulate pulsatile flow.

34
00:01:52,270 --> 00:01:53,920
Veins, on the other hand,

35
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:57,140
return deoxygenated blood
to the heart under low

36
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:00,000
pressure conditions.
They tend to have thinner

37
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,500
walls and less move muscle
compared to arteries,

38
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:07,080
and they often contain
valves to prevent backflow.

39
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:11,380
Capillaries are the
smallest vessels and serve

40
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:13,960
as a site for the
exchange of oxygen,

41
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:17,340
nutrients, and waste
between blood and tissues.

42
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:20,760
Despite these
structural differences,

43
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:23,160
all blood vessels
are lined by a

44
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:25,680
continuous layer of
endothelial cells.

45
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:28,680
These cells are not just
structural, but they

46
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:31,560
also play a dynamic role
in regulating vascular

47
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:36,360
tone, permeability,
inflammation, and thrombosis.

48
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:39,120
Understanding their
function is essential to

49
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:41,620
appreciating how vascular
health is maintained

50
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:44,880
and how dysfunction can
contribute to disease.

51
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:48,840
So let's take a closer look

52
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:50,840
at the vascular structure.

53
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:53,000
So I want to focus
your attention

54
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,340
to the image on the right.

55
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:56,840
At first glance, arteries and

56
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:58,380
veins appear quite similar.

57
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:00,880
Both vessels share
the same three layers.

58
00:03:01,410 --> 00:03:04,360
First, the innermost
layer is known as the

59
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:07,520
tunica interna, which
contains the endothelial

60
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,680
cells forming the layer
called the endothelium.

61
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:13,640
Then, we have the
middle layer, which

62
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:16,700
is known as the
tunica media, which is

63
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:19,260
made primarily of
smooth muscle cells.

64
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:23,040
Lastly, we have the
tunica externa, which

65
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:26,040
is the outermost layer,
which is composed

66
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:28,360
mainly of fibroblasts
that produce

67
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:31,460
collagen and elastin
for structural support.

68
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:34,820
The key difference
between arteries and veins

69
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:37,380
lies in the thickness
of the tunica media.

70
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:40,920
Arteries have a much thicker
smooth muscle layer than

71
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:44,360
veins. And this makes sense
physiologically, since

72
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:47,320
arteries must withstand
and regulate high-pressure

73
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:51,060
blood flow coming directly
from the heart, whereas in

74
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:54,160
veins, they operate under
lower-pressure conditions,

75
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:57,560
and they rely more on valves
and surrounding muscle

76
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:01,180
contractions to assist with
blood return to the heart.

77
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:04,420
Despite this
difference in muscular

78
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:07,020
thickness, both arteries
and veins have an

79
00:04:07,020 --> 00:04:09,960
endothelial lining in
the tunica interna.

80
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:13,380
In the image in the
middle, you can see

81
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,440
that the endothelial
cells are highlighted

82
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:18,120
in green with their
nuclei in blue.

83
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:21,700
What's notable is that the
endothelium is a single

84
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:25,660
monolayer of endothelial
cells. It is not multilayered.

85
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:29,920
This endothelial layer is
not just a passive barrier,

86
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:33,220
but it acts as a semi-permeable
interface allowing the

87
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:36,740
selective exchange of gases,
nutrients, and signaling

88
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:39,420
molecules between the blood
and surrounding tissues.

89
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:41,160
Finally,

90
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:43,600
if we look at the
bottom right, we see

91
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:46,620
capillaries, which are an
even simpler structure.

92
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:50,620
Capillaries are composed
only of endothelial cells,

93
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:55,480
and this structure
facilitates efficient exchange

94
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:59,160
between blood and tissues
at the microvascular level.

95
00:05:01,320 --> 00:05:04,220
So what are endothelial cells?

96
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:08,020
They form a single
monolayer lining

97
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:09,800
the inside of all
blood vessels.

98
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:12,800
Endothelial cells
play a key role in

99
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:15,360
maintaining a vascular
homeostasis by

100
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:18,020
releasing several
signaling molecules.

101
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:21,900
One of the most important
is nitric oxide, a potent

102
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:25,440
vasodilator. This is a
major takeaway from today's

103
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:29,300
lecture, that endothelial cells
produce nitric oxide, which

104
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:32,420
promotes vessel relaxation
and healthy blood flow.

105
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:35,720
They also produce other
factors like process

106
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:38,620
cyclin, which causes
vasodilation, and

107
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:41,360
thromboxane, which
causes vasoconstriction.

108
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:45,120
However, endothelial
cells can also generate

109
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:49,220
reactive oxygen species,
which contribute to oxidative

110
00:05:49,220 --> 00:05:52,920
stress and vascular
damage. In a healthy state,

111
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:56,200
reactive oxygen species
levels are minimal, but

112
00:05:56,200 --> 00:05:59,720
when reactive oxygen species
production exceeds that

113
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:03,300
of nitric oxide, it indicates
endothelial dysfunction,

114
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:06,320
a key contributor to
cardiovascular disease.

115
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:09,720
Today, we'll be
exploring both the normal

116
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:12,820
physiological roles of
endothelial cells and

117
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:15,480
what happens when they
become dysfunctional.

118
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:21,300
Where do you find endothelial
cells? They are found

119
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:24,180
in all blood vessels,
including arteries, veins,

120
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:26,980
and capillaries, and they
compose the inner layer of

121
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:29,590
the blood vessel which
is called the endothelium

122
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,540
here we have two images of
two key cell types found

123
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:41,040
in the vascular wall both
grown in cell culture on

124
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:44,180
the left you see endothelial
cells and on the right

125
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:48,320
you see vascular smooth
muscle cells one of the first

126
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:51,020
differences you'll notice
is in size and shape

127
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:54,720
endothelial cells are smaller
and have a cobblestone

128
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:58,340
shape whereas as smooth
muscle cells are larger and

129
00:06:58,340 --> 00:07:01,480
have a spindle shape with
an elongated appearance.

130
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:04,860
Another key difference
is how they grow.

131
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:09,140
Endothelial cells form
a single monolayer.

132
00:07:09,180 --> 00:07:12,000
When they run out of
space, they stop growing,

133
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:15,060
whereas smooth
muscle cells can grow

134
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:17,120
in multiple layers
stacking on top

135
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:19,140
of each other when
space is limited.

136
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:22,660
For this lecture, we
will only focus on

137
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:25,760
endothelial cells. the
next lecture in this series

138
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:28,160
will explore the
molecular physiology of

139
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:30,520
vascular smooth muscle
cells in more depth.

140
00:07:32,940 --> 00:07:34,800
Let's start with the primary

141
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,360
function of the endothelium.

142
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:39,800
In the image on the top
right, you can see the

143
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:43,260
endothelial layer lining
the inside of the vessel. It

144
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:45,840
serves as a selective
barrier regulating what

145
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:48,720
passes between the blood
and the deeper layers of the

146
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:52,120
vessel wall. But over
time, we have seen that

147
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:55,000
research has shown that the
endothelium is much more

148
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,220
than just a physical
barrier. We know today that

149
00:07:58,220 --> 00:08:00,980
endothelial cells play
active roles in maintaining

150
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:05,060
vascular health through
regulating vasodilation,

151
00:08:05,220 --> 00:08:06,220
inflammation,

152
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:10,000
blood flow, and overall
homeostasis. To give

153
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,720
you a sense of scale,
the human body contains

154
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:15,680
approximately 60 trillion
endothelial cells

155
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:18,620
lining every artery,
vein, and capillary.

156
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:22,300
Looking at the schematic,
you can see a cross-section

157
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:24,860
of a blood vessel in
the bottom right of this

158
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:28,840
slide. The endothelium
lines the inside and beneath

159
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:32,520
it is the smooth muscle
layer. The endothelium is

160
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:35,660
innervated by cholinergic
nerves which release

161
00:08:35,660 --> 00:08:39,580
acetylcholine. This
acetylcholine binds to muscarinic

162
00:08:39,580 --> 00:08:42,720
receptors on the endothelial
cells, triggering the

163
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:46,800
release of nitric oxide,
which leads to vasodilation.

164
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:49,600
The vascular smooth
muscle is also

165
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:51,740
involved in regulating
vessel tone,

166
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:54,780
primarily through
noradrenergic nerves and

167
00:08:54,780 --> 00:08:57,860
epinephrine, which
causes vasoconstriction.

168
00:08:58,010 --> 00:09:00,980
We'll dive deeper into
that in the next lecture.

169
00:09:02,870 --> 00:09:06,240
Here we have a figure
that highlights the wide

170
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:09,220
range of factors produced
by endothelial cells.

171
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:11,940
This slide really
shows how functionally

172
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:13,880
diverse endothelial cells are.

173
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:16,460
They're not just passive
linings, but they

174
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:19,300
actively regulate vascular
tone and homeostasis.

175
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:22,480
For this lecture, I want
you to focus on two key

176
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:26,400
categories, vasodilators
and vasoconstrictors.

177
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:29,860
The main takeaway is that
endothelial cells produce

178
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:33,140
dietric oxide, a gas
that causes vasodilation.

179
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:35,820
They also release
prostacycline,

180
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:39,500
another vasodilator.
On the vasoconstrictor

181
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:41,820
side, they produce thromboxane,

182
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:45,920
endothelin, and reactive
oxygen species, which

183
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:49,080
contribute to oxidative stress
and vascular dysfunction.

184
00:09:49,930 --> 00:09:53,760
Lastly, we also know that
endothelial cells can

185
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:57,520
produce procoagulant factors,
like the Juan-Villebrand

186
00:09:57,520 --> 00:10:01,380
factor, as well as the
platelet-activating factor.

187
00:10:06,150 --> 00:10:08,890
Physiological conditions.
there is a balance

188
00:10:08,890 --> 00:10:12,210
between vasodilation and
vasoconstriction, which

189
00:10:12,210 --> 00:10:14,770
maintains the baseline
tone of blood vessels.

190
00:10:14,930 --> 00:10:18,090
Vessels are not constantly
relaxed or contracted.

191
00:10:18,090 --> 00:10:21,030
They stay in a regulated
state of partial tone.

192
00:10:21,030 --> 00:10:24,530
This balance is maintained
by opposing factors.

193
00:10:24,790 --> 00:10:26,990
On the vasodilation side,

194
00:10:27,070 --> 00:10:30,770
the key player is nitric
oxide and prostacyclin.

195
00:10:30,770 --> 00:10:34,310
On the vasoconstriction
side, we have endothelin,

196
00:10:34,570 --> 00:10:38,270
we have thalboxane and
reactive oxygen species.

197
00:10:38,790 --> 00:10:41,030
When the balance is disrupted,

198
00:10:41,030 --> 00:10:42,570
such as in disease states,

199
00:10:42,690 --> 00:10:44,610
vascular tone becomes abnormal,

200
00:10:44,610 --> 00:10:47,310
leading to vascular
dysfunction.

201
00:10:49,510 --> 00:10:52,590
When we have
endothelial dysfunction,

202
00:10:52,710 --> 00:10:55,310
it means that the
endothelial cells are no

203
00:10:55,310 --> 00:10:58,710
longer functioning properly.
They begin to produce

204
00:10:58,710 --> 00:11:02,010
less nitric oxide and
more vasoconstrictors,

205
00:11:02,350 --> 00:11:04,910
disrupting this
normal balance. This

206
00:11:04,910 --> 00:11:06,730
leads to vessel
constriction and can

207
00:11:06,730 --> 00:11:09,370
contribute to vascular
complications.

208
00:11:09,690 --> 00:11:12,950
One major consequence
is vascular stiffening,

209
00:11:12,950 --> 00:11:15,710
where the vessel loses
its elasticity and

210
00:11:15,710 --> 00:11:18,610
becomes rigid. We'll
explore this more in the

211
00:11:18,610 --> 00:11:21,630
upcoming lecture on
hemodynamics and circulation.

212
00:11:22,350 --> 00:11:25,070
It's also important
to note that nitric

213
00:11:25,070 --> 00:11:27,270
oxide is not just
a vasodilator,

214
00:11:27,270 --> 00:11:29,970
but also an anti
-proliferative factor.

215
00:11:30,110 --> 00:11:33,670
Under normal conditions,
it keeps vascular smooth

216
00:11:33,670 --> 00:11:37,170
muscle cells from proliferating,
but when the nitric

217
00:11:37,170 --> 00:11:39,830
oxide levels drop, this
protective effect is

218
00:11:39,830 --> 00:11:43,290
lost, leading to smooth
muscle cell proliferation,

219
00:11:43,290 --> 00:11:46,590
which contributes further
to vascular dysfunction.

220
00:11:49,950 --> 00:11:53,210
Endothelial cells release
several vasodietary

221
00:11:53,210 --> 00:11:55,910
factors, collectively
known as endothelium

222
00:11:55,910 --> 00:11:59,850
-derived relaxation
factors, or EDRFs. While

223
00:11:59,850 --> 00:12:02,050
not all of them have
been fully identified,

224
00:12:02,290 --> 00:12:03,790
one of the most well-known and

225
00:12:03,790 --> 00:12:05,870
well-studied is nitric oxide.

226
00:12:06,790 --> 00:12:10,050
Acetylcholine, a
neurotransmitter, is released by

227
00:12:10,050 --> 00:12:14,190
cholinergic nerves, and
acetylcholine will bind to muscarinic

228
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:17,010
receptors on endothelial
cells, triggering the

229
00:12:17,010 --> 00:12:20,390
release of nitric oxide,
which then causes relaxation.

230
00:12:20,950 --> 00:12:24,870
The image on the bottom
shows a common experiment we

231
00:12:24,870 --> 00:12:28,210
do in Dr. Sepulveda's lab
to demonstrate this process.

232
00:12:28,210 --> 00:12:31,850
We isolate a blood
vessel and are able to

233
00:12:31,850 --> 00:12:35,690
contract it using
phenylephrine, and then we are

234
00:12:35,690 --> 00:12:40,170
able to apply acetylcholine,
which then relaxes

235
00:12:40,170 --> 00:12:42,990
the vessel because
the endothelial cells

236
00:12:42,990 --> 00:12:46,330
release nitric oxide in
response to the stimulus.

237
00:12:47,050 --> 00:12:49,990
But what happens
if this endothelial

238
00:12:49,990 --> 00:12:52,690
layer is damaged or removed.

239
00:12:56,590 --> 00:13:00,790
We can repeat the same question
to answer this question,

240
00:13:00,790 --> 00:13:03,990
and by contracting the
vessel with phenylephrine

241
00:13:03,990 --> 00:13:08,130
and then adding acetylcholine,
but this time with the

242
00:13:08,130 --> 00:13:11,690
endothelium removed, the
result is very different. The

243
00:13:11,690 --> 00:13:15,210
vessel no longer relaxes in
response to acetylcholine.

244
00:13:15,330 --> 00:13:16,290
Why?

245
00:13:16,490 --> 00:13:20,230
Because without endothelial
cells, acetylcholine

246
00:13:20,230 --> 00:13:23,270
has no myrskirinic
disruptors to bind to,

247
00:13:23,270 --> 00:13:25,910
and therefore there's no
nitric oxide released.

248
00:13:26,090 --> 00:13:29,630
This experiment shows
that without endothelial

249
00:13:29,630 --> 00:13:32,650
cells, there's no
nitric oxide production,

250
00:13:32,650 --> 00:13:36,430
and therefore no vessel
relaxation occurs.

251
00:13:39,690 --> 00:13:43,390
This was a major discovery
in vascular biology.

252
00:13:43,390 --> 00:13:46,030
one that changed our
understanding of how blood

253
00:13:46,030 --> 00:13:49,430
vessels function and ultimately
led three scientists,

254
00:13:49,430 --> 00:13:51,510
one of which is from New York,

255
00:13:51,510 --> 00:13:54,690
to win the Nobel Prize in 1998.

256
00:13:54,790 --> 00:13:57,670
The discovery came
somewhat unexpectedly.

257
00:13:57,710 --> 00:14:01,630
In their lab, experiments
with isolated blood vessels

258
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:05,550
didn't seem to relax in
response to acetylcholine.

259
00:14:05,550 --> 00:14:08,390
And after some investigation,
what they realized

260
00:14:08,390 --> 00:14:10,930
was that the technician
had accidentally been

261
00:14:10,930 --> 00:14:14,010
removing the endothelial
layer during the preparation.

262
00:14:14,010 --> 00:14:17,630
And without the endothelium,
we know that no nitric

263
00:14:17,630 --> 00:14:21,150
oxide can be produced and
no vasodilation can occur.

264
00:14:21,490 --> 00:14:24,010
This led to the
breakthrough finding that

265
00:14:24,010 --> 00:14:26,230
endothelial cells are
essential for nitric

266
00:14:26,230 --> 00:14:29,270
oxide production and that
nitric oxide is a key

267
00:14:29,270 --> 00:14:32,050
signaling molecule in
vascular regulation.

268
00:14:32,370 --> 00:14:35,450
This discovery paved the
way for many therapeutic

269
00:14:35,450 --> 00:14:38,430
applications, including
drugs that enhance

270
00:14:38,430 --> 00:14:41,830
nitric oxide signaling.
One well-known example

271
00:14:41,830 --> 00:14:44,890
is ******, which we'll
discuss shortly. As

272
00:14:51,150 --> 00:14:53,670
I mentioned earlier, the
key takeaway from this

273
00:14:53,670 --> 00:14:57,310
lecture is nitric oxide
signaling. In this schematic

274
00:14:57,310 --> 00:15:00,310
on the left, we see
endothelial cells lining the

275
00:15:00,310 --> 00:15:03,390
vessel with vascular smooth
muscles adjacent to them.

276
00:15:03,730 --> 00:15:07,610
This process begins
when cholinergic nerves

277
00:15:07,610 --> 00:15:10,730
release acetylcholine,
which then binds to the

278
00:15:10,730 --> 00:15:14,170
muscarinic M3 receptors
on the endothelial cells.

279
00:15:14,330 --> 00:15:18,370
This binding triggers an
increase in intracellular

280
00:15:18,370 --> 00:15:21,570
calcium, which then
binds to calmodulin.

281
00:15:21,950 --> 00:15:26,090
The calcium calmodulin
complex activates the

282
00:15:26,090 --> 00:15:29,750
enzyme enos, which is
known as endothelial nitric

283
00:15:29,750 --> 00:15:35,290
oxide synthase, and then
this enzyme enos converts

284
00:15:35,290 --> 00:15:39,430
L-arginine into nitric
oxide, a gas that diffuses

285
00:15:39,430 --> 00:15:42,530
into the nearby vascular
smooth muscle cells.

286
00:15:42,990 --> 00:15:44,330
There,

287
00:15:44,570 --> 00:15:47,610
nitric oxide activates
guanylate cyclase,

288
00:15:47,610 --> 00:15:51,970
which converts GTP
into cyclic GMP.

289
00:15:52,290 --> 00:15:56,890
Cyclic GMP then activates
protein kinase G,

290
00:15:57,070 --> 00:16:00,630
abbreviated as PKG,
leading to smooth

291
00:16:00,630 --> 00:16:03,090
muscle relaxation
and vasodilation.

292
00:16:03,670 --> 00:16:07,610
A few key points to
remember is that one,

293
00:16:07,610 --> 00:16:10,490
calcium is essential
for E-NOS activation,

294
00:16:10,690 --> 00:16:11,670
two,

295
00:16:12,010 --> 00:16:13,750
L-arginine is the substrate

296
00:16:13,750 --> 00:16:15,910
for nitric oxide production,

297
00:16:16,230 --> 00:16:19,290
and three, nitric
oxide diffuses into

298
00:16:19,290 --> 00:16:21,210
the smooth muscle
and triggers a

299
00:16:21,210 --> 00:16:24,170
cyclic GMP-dependent
signaling cascade.

300
00:16:26,950 --> 00:16:30,230
Here I've included a
picture of an L-arginine

301
00:16:30,230 --> 00:16:33,570
supplement as a memory tool
for you all, and you may

302
00:16:33,570 --> 00:16:36,090
have seen this supplement
in your local pharmacy

303
00:16:36,090 --> 00:16:39,870
marketed for blood flow
and vascular health. That's

304
00:16:39,870 --> 00:16:42,730
because L-arginine
feeds this very pathway,

305
00:16:42,730 --> 00:16:46,110
supporting nitric oxide
production and vasodilation.

306
00:16:46,830 --> 00:16:49,510
Now we'll take a
look at the enzyme

307
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:52,720
PDE5, which stands
for phosphodiesterase

308
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:56,010
type 5, which breaks
down cyclic GMP.

309
00:16:56,170 --> 00:17:00,330
When cyclic GMP is degraded,
protein kinase G is

310
00:17:00,330 --> 00:17:03,510
no longer activated, and
as a result, vasodilation

311
00:17:03,510 --> 00:17:07,230
is reduced. Researchers
realized that by inhibiting

312
00:17:07,230 --> 00:17:11,350
PDE5, they could preserve
cyclic GMP levels,

313
00:17:11,350 --> 00:17:15,030
sustaining the protein
kinase G activation and

314
00:17:15,030 --> 00:17:18,330
promoting relaxation of
vascular smooth muscle.

315
00:17:18,330 --> 00:17:22,790
That's exactly the mechanism
of ******. ****** is a

316
00:17:22,790 --> 00:17:26,370
PDE5 inhibitor, and it
blocks the breakdown of C

317
00:17:26,370 --> 00:17:30,290
-GMP, allowing for more
vasodilation to occur by

318
00:17:30,290 --> 00:17:33,310
keeping the nitric oxide
signaling pathway active.

319
00:17:36,550 --> 00:17:39,790
Here is a quick summary
of the nitric oxide

320
00:17:39,790 --> 00:17:42,730
signaling cascade. In
healthy endothelial

321
00:17:42,730 --> 00:17:45,490
cells, nitric oxide
production leads to several

322
00:17:45,490 --> 00:17:49,130
protective effects,
vasodilation, reduced

323
00:17:49,130 --> 00:17:51,790
smooth muscle contraction
and proliferation,

324
00:17:52,330 --> 00:17:54,690
decreased platelet aggregation,

325
00:17:54,910 --> 00:17:58,010
lower endothelin
production, and reduce

326
00:17:58,010 --> 00:18:02,070
oxidation of low-density
lipoprotein, or LDL.

327
00:18:02,350 --> 00:18:04,670
Together, these effects help

328
00:18:04,670 --> 00:18:06,210
maintain vascular health.

329
00:18:07,290 --> 00:18:09,810
But when the
endothelial cells become

330
00:18:09,810 --> 00:18:12,960
dysfunctional, nitric
oxide production decreases,

331
00:18:12,960 --> 00:18:15,210
and as a result, we
see the opposite.

332
00:18:15,250 --> 00:18:17,570
Increased vasoconstriction,

333
00:18:17,670 --> 00:18:19,670
smooth muscle proliferation,

334
00:18:20,050 --> 00:18:22,050
more platelet aggregation,

335
00:18:22,280 --> 00:18:25,490
higher endothelin
levels, and greater

336
00:18:25,490 --> 00:18:28,030
low density
lipoprotein oxidation.

337
00:18:28,110 --> 00:18:30,790
All of these changes
contribute to

338
00:18:30,790 --> 00:18:32,970
vascular damage and
disease progression.

339
00:18:36,730 --> 00:18:39,850
This slide summarizes
the mechanisms by which

340
00:18:39,850 --> 00:18:42,410
endothelial cells cause
vascular relaxation.

341
00:18:42,410 --> 00:18:45,630
We've already covered the
main pathway in the middle.

342
00:18:46,290 --> 00:18:49,210
For your purposes, I'll
summarize it one more

343
00:18:49,210 --> 00:18:52,390
time. So, the first
thing that happens is

344
00:18:52,390 --> 00:18:55,470
acetylcholine binds to
muscarinic receptors on

345
00:18:55,470 --> 00:18:58,350
endothelial cells.
This then increases

346
00:18:58,350 --> 00:19:01,890
intracellular calcium,
which binds to calmodulin.

347
00:19:02,230 --> 00:19:07,270
The calcium calmodulin
complex activates ENOS, which

348
00:19:07,270 --> 00:19:10,850
is an enzyme that stands
for endothelial nitric oxide

349
00:19:10,850 --> 00:19:15,070
synthase, which converts L
-arginine to nitric oxide.

350
00:19:15,550 --> 00:19:18,370
The nitric oxide
then diffuses to the

351
00:19:18,370 --> 00:19:20,450
smooth muscle,
activating cyclic

352
00:19:20,450 --> 00:19:23,870
GMP and leading to
vessel relaxation.

353
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:26,750
But it's also
important to note that

354
00:19:26,750 --> 00:19:28,430
there are additional
pathways that also

355
00:19:28,430 --> 00:19:30,610
contribute to smooth
muscle relaxation.

356
00:19:30,970 --> 00:19:34,510
On the left, we see
prostacyclin, another factor

357
00:19:34,510 --> 00:19:37,010
released by endothelial
cells, which promotes

358
00:19:37,010 --> 00:19:40,350
vasodilation through
cyclic AMP signaling.

359
00:19:40,770 --> 00:19:45,100
On the right, we see the
factor endothelium-derived

360
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:49,170
hyperpolarizing factor, which
causes hyperpolarization of

361
00:19:49,170 --> 00:19:52,870
smooth muscle cells, which
also leads to relaxation.

362
00:19:53,010 --> 00:19:55,810
For this lecture,
the main focus is the

363
00:19:55,810 --> 00:19:58,250
pathway in the middle,
which we have discussed.

364
00:20:03,250 --> 00:20:05,910
Try our first practice
question for today.

365
00:20:05,910 --> 00:20:08,450
Please take a moment
to pause the video and

366
00:20:08,450 --> 00:20:11,250
answer the question before
we review the answer.

367
00:20:14,950 --> 00:20:17,290
The correct answer is D,

368
00:20:17,970 --> 00:20:20,510
IM epinephrine administration.

369
00:20:20,810 --> 00:20:23,950
So in this case, the
patient is showing

370
00:20:23,950 --> 00:20:27,150
signs of anaphylaxis, a
severe and potentially

371
00:20:27,150 --> 00:20:29,130
life-threatening
allergic reaction.

372
00:20:29,330 --> 00:20:31,490
Epinephrine is the
first-line treatment

373
00:20:31,490 --> 00:20:34,190
for anaphylaxis, and
one of the ways this

374
00:20:34,190 --> 00:20:37,230
medication works is
by vasoconstriction.

375
00:20:37,590 --> 00:20:40,950
Let's talk a little bit
more about epinephrine.

376
00:20:40,950 --> 00:20:44,670
People with severe allergies
often carry an epinephrine

377
00:20:44,670 --> 00:20:47,110
auto-injector like an
EpiPen for emergencies.

378
00:20:47,690 --> 00:20:51,110
In a severe allergic
reaction, there's a surge of

379
00:20:51,110 --> 00:20:54,570
histamine and bradykinin,
which triggers vasodilation

380
00:20:54,570 --> 00:20:56,870
and leads to a drop
in the blood pressure

381
00:20:56,870 --> 00:21:00,370
by increasing the nitric
oxide production of

382
00:21:00,370 --> 00:21:04,930
endothelial cells. To reverse
this, we give epinephrine,

383
00:21:04,930 --> 00:21:07,810
which activates alpha
-adrenergic receptors on

384
00:21:07,810 --> 00:21:10,870
vascular smooth muscle,
causing vasoconstriction,

385
00:21:10,870 --> 00:21:13,690
and ultimately helps
