386
00:21:15,630 --> 00:21:19,010
Now we will shift gears
and discuss the clinical

387
00:21:19,010 --> 00:21:21,410
implications of
endothelial dysfunction.

388
00:21:21,410 --> 00:21:23,930
I have highlighted a
few here that we'll

389
00:21:23,930 --> 00:21:26,370
briefly touch upon
in today's session.

390
00:21:29,070 --> 00:21:32,470
Juan-Villebrand disease is
a common genetic bleeding

391
00:21:32,470 --> 00:21:35,670
disorder caused by low
levels or dysfunctional

392
00:21:35,670 --> 00:21:38,810
Juan-Villebrand factor.
This protein is produced

393
00:21:38,810 --> 00:21:42,070
exclusively by endothelial
cells, which makes it a

394
00:21:42,070 --> 00:21:45,230
key marker of endothelial
function in coagulation.

395
00:21:45,690 --> 00:21:49,990
In a normal physiological
response, when a blood vessel

396
00:21:49,990 --> 00:21:53,410
is injured, the endothelial
cells near the injury site

397
00:21:53,410 --> 00:21:56,770
release Juan-Vilibrand
factor into the bloodstream.

398
00:21:57,010 --> 00:22:01,030
This Juan-Vilibrand factor
binds to the exposed collagen

399
00:22:01,030 --> 00:22:04,590
in the damaged vessel wall
and acts like a bridge,

400
00:22:04,590 --> 00:22:07,790
helping platelets adhere
to the site of the injury.

401
00:22:08,250 --> 00:22:11,490
Platelets can then
aggregate and form a plug,

402
00:22:11,490 --> 00:22:14,370
which is the first step
to stopping the bleeding.

403
00:22:15,010 --> 00:22:18,090
However, in Juan
-Vilibrand disease, this is

404
00:22:18,090 --> 00:22:20,770
not the case because
endothelial cells either

405
00:22:20,770 --> 00:22:23,830
don't produce enough
von-villibrand factor or

406
00:22:23,830 --> 00:22:26,390
the von-villibrand
factor is not functional

407
00:22:26,770 --> 00:22:30,290
and so they're the initial
platelet adhesion step

408
00:22:30,290 --> 00:22:33,530
is impaired and as a
result the platelet plug

409
00:22:33,530 --> 00:22:36,190
does not form properly
and bleeding continues

410
00:22:36,190 --> 00:22:39,740
longer than it should even
with minor injuries the

411
00:22:39,740 --> 00:22:43,050
standard treatment or
von-villibrand disease is

412
00:22:43,050 --> 00:22:46,230
desmopressin which works
by stimulating endothelial

413
00:22:46,230 --> 00:22:49,270
cells to release dork
-wan-villibrand factor.

414
00:22:51,300 --> 00:22:53,430
Hypertension is
both a cause and

415
00:22:53,430 --> 00:22:55,350
consequence of
endothelial dysfunction.

416
00:22:55,510 --> 00:22:58,570
When the endothelium
becomes dysfunctional, it

417
00:22:58,570 --> 00:23:02,050
produces less nitric oxide,
which leads to increased

418
00:23:02,050 --> 00:23:05,130
vasoconstriction, higher
vascular tone, and

419
00:23:05,130 --> 00:23:07,710
ultimately contributes to
elevated blood pressure.

420
00:23:07,930 --> 00:23:11,830
At the same time, high blood
pressure itself increases

421
00:23:11,830 --> 00:23:14,930
shear stress on the vessel
walls, and this mechanical

422
00:23:14,930 --> 00:23:17,990
force can further damage
the endothelial layer.

423
00:23:20,050 --> 00:23:23,730
Let's take a closer look
at shear stress. Under

424
00:23:23,730 --> 00:23:26,330
normal conditions, we
see that there's laminar

425
00:23:26,330 --> 00:23:28,750
blood flow, which applies
a steady protective

426
00:23:28,750 --> 00:23:32,110
force on endothelial
cells. But in hypertension,

427
00:23:32,290 --> 00:23:34,990
the increased blood flow
and pressure generate

428
00:23:34,990 --> 00:23:37,270
excessive shear stress,
which can damage the

429
00:23:37,270 --> 00:23:40,730
endothelial layer. Once
damaged, endothelial cells

430
00:23:40,730 --> 00:23:43,710
produce less nitric oxide
leading to vasoconstriction

431
00:23:43,710 --> 00:23:45,970
and further elevation
of blood pressure.

432
00:23:46,130 --> 00:23:50,450
As shown in the image on
the left, we see that when

433
00:23:50,450 --> 00:23:53,190
the blood flow becomes
turbulent or too strong,

434
00:23:53,190 --> 00:23:56,550
it disrupts endothelial
function, reducing nitric

435
00:23:56,550 --> 00:24:00,150
oxide production and
contributing to the cycle of

436
00:24:00,150 --> 00:24:02,970
vascular dysfunction that
can lead to hypertension.

437
00:24:06,290 --> 00:24:08,810
Let's briefly discuss
how blood flow

438
00:24:08,810 --> 00:24:11,750
dynamics work across
the vascular system.

439
00:24:11,890 --> 00:24:14,810
One key principle to
remember is that blood

440
00:24:14,810 --> 00:24:17,410
flow velocity is
inversely related to the

441
00:24:17,410 --> 00:24:20,290
total cross-sectional
area of the vessel type.

442
00:24:20,510 --> 00:24:24,170
The aorta has a small
total cross-sectional area,

443
00:24:24,170 --> 00:24:26,970
so the blood flow
velocity is highest there.

444
00:24:27,370 --> 00:24:30,970
In contrast, capillaries,
though tiny individually,

445
00:24:31,230 --> 00:24:34,060
collectively have the
largest cross-sectional area,

446
00:24:34,060 --> 00:24:37,170
so the blood flow is slowest
at the capillary level

447
00:24:37,170 --> 00:24:40,530
this slower flow in
capillaries is essential for

448
00:24:40,530 --> 00:24:43,550
efficient exchange of
oxygen and nutrients between

449
00:24:43,550 --> 00:24:47,230
blood and tissue which
makes sense we'll cover this

450
00:24:47,230 --> 00:24:50,170
more in detail in the
hemodynamics and circulation

451
00:24:50,170 --> 00:24:53,090
lecture but it's important
to understand the basic

452
00:24:53,090 --> 00:25:00,070
concepts now another concept
we'll explore in the next

453
00:25:00,070 --> 00:25:03,090
lecture is total peripheral
resistance but I want

454
00:25:03,090 --> 00:25:05,850
to briefly introduce it
here. Total peripheral

455
00:25:05,850 --> 00:25:09,130
resistance refers to the
resistance that blood encounters

456
00:25:09,130 --> 00:25:12,070
as it flows through the
systemic circulation, and

457
00:25:12,070 --> 00:25:14,450
it plays a key role in
regulating blood pressure.

458
00:25:14,670 --> 00:25:17,670
We'll see later that blood
pressure is equal to the

459
00:25:17,670 --> 00:25:20,650
